The main objective here is to investigate an elliptic equation of Kirchhoff type that contains an inhomogeneous non-Laplacian term, given by $\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
\displaystyle \beta(\|u|\,^{2})u-\Delta u=|u|^{q^2-2} & \text{in} \ \ D,\quad u=0 \quad \text{on} \ \ \partial D,
\end{array}
\right.$ where $\beta$ is a function that approaches zero in a sublinear manner as the input value decreases without bound, and $q$ is a critical exponent resulting from the Moser-Trudinger inequality under certain constraints on the domain $D$. To tackle this problem, new technical constraints are established on the function $\beta$ that are stronger than those identified in earlier works, and the issue is resolved through a novel approach that utilizes a singular version of the Trudinger-Moser inequality.

This paper investigates the standardized existence of a ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
\begin{split}
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
\Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
\quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
\end{array}
\right.
\end{split} \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and the given functions $f$ and $F$. Nonetheless, when $\beta$ falls within a particular range that satisfies $\beta\in (0, \frac{1}{4})$, we are able to establish the standardized existence of a ground state solution via variational methods.

